extism-endive — Extism's Java host SDK, ported to Endive
========================================================

This module is a vendored copy of the `core/` module of

    https://github.com/extism/chicory-sdk   at commit 8bceda1 (2025-10-31)

Copyright 2022-2024 Dylibso, Inc., distributed under the BSD 3-Clause licence
(see LICENSE, the upstream text verbatim). The upstream module's own tests are
not ported: they need fixtures this repository does not have. What we use is
covered by function-host's tests (docs/spec/function-wasm-runtime.md §6).

The package stays `org.extism.sdk.chicory` so that a future upstream release on
Endive can replace this module wholesale.

Changes from upstream (every one; each changed line is also marked
"FlowCatalyst change" in the source):

1. Runtime: every `com.dylibso.chicory.*` import rewritten to `run.endive.*`
   (Endive 1.1.0 is the renamed continuation of Chicory). Mechanical; nothing
   else changed by this step.
2. Removed `JacksonJsonCodec` and `JakartaJsonCodec` (Jackson 2 / Jakarta JSON)
   and the `JdkHttpClientAdapter` / `HttpUrlConnectionClientAdapter` client
   adapters. Upstream they are already deprecated-for-removal stubs that throw;
   the host supplies its own codec and adapter through `HttpConfig`
   (org.extism.sdk:http-api).
3. `ManifestWasm.fromModule(WasmModule)` (+ `ManifestWasmModule`, and its branch
   in `ChicoryModule.fromWasm`): an already-parsed module. Upstream re-parses
   the module on every `CompiledPlugin.instantiate()`, so a machine factory
   keyed by module identity (`CachedAotMachineFactory`) compiles it again —
   new classes, new metaspace — for every instance. With a shared module a
   host compiles once per module and every instance shares the compiled code.
4. `Kernel`: the Extism kernel (`extism-runtime.wasm`) is parsed once per
   process (`Kernel.module()`), for the same reason as 3.
5. `HostEnv.Http.headers()`: encode the adapter's response headers. Upstream
   passed `Map.of()` to the codec, so a guest never saw a response header even
   with `withHttpResponseHeaders(true)`.
6. The build: compiled at the reactor's Java release; LICENSE and this NOTICE
   are packaged under META-INF/.
- Kernel memory cap: `Kernel` accepts a maximum page count for the kernel's own linear memory
  (input, output and guest-allocated blocks), applied through `Manifest.Options
  .withKernelMemoryMaxPages`; the initial size stays the kernel module's declared minimum.
  Upstream leaves it uncapped.
